    "['set protocols ospf area [area_id]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ospf 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Huawei equivalent for the OSPF interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\nospf 1\\n area 0.0.0.0\\n  network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0\\n  network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255\\n  network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Huawei, OSPF networks are configured under the area using network statements rather than per-interface\\n2. The mask uses wildcard bits (inverse of subnet mask)\\n3. The loopback (lo0) and physical interfaces are all included in area 0.0.0.0 via their respective network ranges\\n4. OSPF process 1 is created (Juniper doesn't require explicit process numbers)\", 'trans_template': 'ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'area 0.0.0.0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'area 0.0.0.0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Huawei equivalent for the OSPF interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\nospf 1\\n area 0.0.0.0\\n  network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0\\n  network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255\\n  network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Huawei, OSPF networks are configured under the area using network statements rather than per-interface\\n2. The mask uses wildcard bits (inverse of subnet mask)\\n3. The loopback (lo0) and physical interfaces are all included in area 0.0.0.0 via their respective network ranges\\n4. OSPF process 1 is created (Juniper doesn't require explicit process numbers)\", 'trans_template': 'area 0.0.0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Huawei equivalent for the OSPF interface configuration:\\n\\n##\\nospf 1\\n area 0.0.0.0\\n  network 10.35.5.9 0.0.0.0\\n  network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255\\n  network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Huawei, OSPF networks are configured under the area